This assignment is to be done in groups established via assignment 2.
The corresponding Lab unit on Port Scanning is available in Week 12.
The purpose of this assignment is to detect open Internet ports on a remote host and to extract information.
For this assignment write a program that tries to open UDP and TCP ports on a remote host. The remote host has some ports open and will respond to requests. The port numbers are not known and will vary over time. If you detect an open port, try to connect and communicate. Capture any response from the remote host.
The general strategy is to first find an open UPD port, send a query and get a secret key. Then find an open TCP port, send a query and get an encrypted message. Finally decrypt the message with the secret key.
Specifications:
use TCP and UDP socket communication
the remote host to scan is blitz.cs.niu.edu (NIU local IP address 10.158.56.43)
only scan ports from 9000 to 9100
if you are able to connect, send query: "group x" ( x is your group number )
capture the response from the remote host: secret key (UPD) or message (TCP)
the server will send an error message, i.e. a plain text message that starts with "Error", in case of a problem
the secret key is 16 bytes long for a key strength of 128bits
decrypt the message with RC4 using the secret key
Notes:
write your program in C++ on your personal Linux system
the open ports on blitz.cs.niu.edu (10.158.56.43) are only reachable from within the NIU network: either run your program while at NIU, or establish a VPN connection to NIU: https://www.niu.edu/doit/services/network/vpn.shtml
blitz.cs.niu.edu also has a public IP address: 131.156.224.116. Please don't use it.
Deliverables:
your program source code file
a PDF file listing 3 responses with the following details:
time of connection, port numbers (UDP, TCP)
decrypted message from remote host
